  "content": {
    "id": "p27-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Initially a wound has an area of $8\\text{ cm}^2$ and after 2 days, the area is $6\\text{ cm}^2$. Assume the area of the wound is decaying exponentially. How long will it take, to the nearest day, for the area of the wound to be 10% of its initial size?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "16 days.",
      "worked": "Let $A(t)$ be the wound area in square centimetres, $t$ days after the initial measurement. The area is multiplied by $\\frac{6}{8}=\\frac34$ every 2 days, so\n$$A(t)=8\\left(\\frac34\\right)^{t/2}.$$\n10% of the initial area is $0.8\\text{ cm}^2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\n8\\left(\\frac34\\right)^{t/2}&=0.8\\\\\n\\left(\\frac34\\right)^{t/2}&=0.1\\\\\n\\log_{10}\\left[\\left(\\frac34\\right)^{t/2}\\right]&=\\log_{10}0.1\\\\\n\\frac{t}{2}\\log_{10}\\frac34&=-1\\\\\nt&=\\frac{-2}{\\log_{10}(3/4)}\\\\\nt&\\approx16.0078\n\\end{align*}$$\nIt will take approximately 16 days, to the nearest day.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 85
  },
